        teacherNotes: "Open screen share, playthis weekBoardWeike（3 example problem, each 2 min, total 6 min）. BoardWeike example 1: 2x + 1 = 7 is an equation. Check whether x=3 is the solution. Solution: This is an equation (uses =). Substitute x=3: 2(3)+1 = 6+1 = 7. Both sides are equal (left side = 7, right side = 7), so x=3 is the solution. Answer: Yes, x=3 is the solution of 2x+1=7. BoardWeike example 2: 2x + 1 > 5 is an inequality. Check whether x=2, x=3, and x=4 are solutions. Solution: This is an inequality (uses >). Substitute x=2: 2(2)+1 = 5. Is 5>5 true? No, 5 is not greater than 5 (5=5). So x=2 is not a solution. Substitute x=3: 2(3)+1 = 7. Is 7>5 true? Yes, 7 is greater than 5. So x=3 is a solution. Substitute x=4: 2(4)+1 = 9. Is 9>5 true? Yes, 9 is greater than 5. So x=4 is a solution. Answer: x=2 is not a solution. x=3 is a solution. x=4 is also a solution. An inequality can have many solutions. BoardWeike example 3: 3x − 2 ≤ 10. Check whether x=4 and x=5 are solutions. Solution: This is an inequality (uses ≤). Substitute x=4: 3(4)−2 = 12−2 = 10. Is 10≤10 true? Yes, 10 is less than or equal to 10 (because 10=10). So x=4 is a solution. Substitute x=5: 3(5)−2 = 15−2 = 13. Is 13≤10 true? No, 13 is not less than or equal to 10 (13>10). So x=5 is not a solution. Answer: x=4 is a solution. x=5 is not a solution. Note: The symbol ≤ means 'less than OR equal to', so if the left side equals the right side, the inequality is still true. play后, Ask the child: 「Can you tell me: what is the difference between < and ≤?'期待answer: '< means less than. ≤ means less than or equal to.'Ask the child: 「For the inequality 2x+1>5, we found that x=3 and x=4 are both solutions. Why can an inequality have more than one solution?'期待answer: 'Because an inequality uses > or < or ≤ or ≥, not =. Many values can make the inequality true. For an equation, only one value makes both sides equal.'"
