        teacherNotes: "givechild 5 problemhavethey/theyat纸abovecalculate（teachernotgivecompleteanswer, onlyGive prompts, havechild自己writeout working steps）: \n\npractice 1: For x + y = 9 and x − y = 3, solve using elimination. (prompts: Add the two equations to eliminate y. Then find x and y.) Expected answer: Add: (x+y)+(x−y)=9+3 → 2x=12 → x=6. Substitute into x+y=9: 6+y=9 → y=3. Solution: (6, 3) ✓. Check: 6+3=9 ✓, 6−3=3 ✓.\n\npractice 2: For y = x + 1 and 2x + y = 10, solve using substitution. (prompts: y = x + 1 is already solved for y. Substitute into the second equation.) Expected answer: Substitute y=x+1 into 2x+y=10: 2x+(x+1)=10 → 3x+1=10 → 3x=9 → x=3. Then y=3+1=4. Solution: (3, 4) ✓. Check: 4=3+1 ✓, 2(3)+4=10 ✓.\n\npractice 3: For 2x + y = 11 and x + y = 7, solve using elimination. (prompts: Subtract the second from the first to eliminate y.) Expected answer: Subtract: (2x+y)−(x+y)=11−7 → x=4. Substitute into x+y=7: 4+y=7 → y=3. Solution: (4, 3) ✓. Check: 2(4)+3=11 ✓, 4+3=7 ✓.\n\npractice 4: For 3x + y = 12 and x + y = 6, can you solve using elimination? (prompts: Subtract to eliminate y. Then find x and y.) Expected answer: Subtract: (3x+y)−(x+y)=12−6 → 2x=6 → x=3. Substitute into x+y=6: 3+y=6 → y=3. Solution: (3, 3) ✓. Check: 3(3)+3=12 ✓, 3+3=6 ✓.\n\npractice 5: For x + y = 7 and x − y = 1, solve using elimination. (prompts: Add to eliminate y.) Expected answer: Add: (x+y)+(x−y)=7+1 → 2x=8 → x=4. Substitute into x+y=7: 4+y=7 → y=3. Solution: (4, 3) ✓. Check: 4+3=7 ✓, 4−3=1 ✓.\n\nIfchildask'Can I use substitution for practice 1?', answer: 「Yes, both methods work. But the item says 'use elimination', so please use elimination for practice. In the real exam, if the item doesn't specify, you can choose whichever method is easier.'",
