        teacherNotes: "Ask the child: 'Today we learn congruent figures（allwaitfigure）. What does 'congruent' mean? Congruent figures have the same shape and the same size. 'Same shape' means they are the same type of figure (both triangles, both rectangles, both circles, etc.). 'Same size' means all corresponding sides are equal and all corresponding angles are equal. For example: Triangle A has sides 3 cm, 4 cm, 5 cm. Triangle B has sides 3 cm, 4 cm, 5 cm. Are they congruent? Yes. Same shape (both triangles), same size (3 = 3, 4 = 4, 5 = 5). They are congruent. Now, what if Triangle B is rotated 90°? Are they still congruent? Yes. Rotation does not change the shape or size. A rotated triangle is still congruent to the original. What if Triangle B is reflected (mirror image)? Are they still congruent? Yes. Reflection does not change the shape or size. A reflected triangle is still congruent to the original. So: rotation and reflection do NOT change congruence. Now, what if Rectangle A is 6 cm by 4 cm, and Rectangle B is 9 cm by 6 cm? Are they congruent? No. Same shape (both rectangles), but different size (6 ≠ 9, 4 ≠ 6). They are NOT congruent. Same shape but different size is NOT congruent. That is called 'similar' (we will learn similar figures next week in G2.2). This week, congruent = same shape AND same size. Rotation/reflection OK. Same shape different size = NOT congruent (that is similar, next week). Same area does not mean congruent. Integer lengths and angles only. No calculator.'",
