        teacherNotes: "Ask the child: 'Today we learn enlargement and reduction of a plane figure using a scale factor（usescaleratio进行平面figure放大 and 缩小）. Last week (Week 77) we learned G2.3 properties of similar triangles and polygons: corresponding angles equal, corresponding sides proportional. This week (Week 78): G2.4 enlargement and reduction. Official content: scale factor k > 1 = enlargement (放大); 0 < k < 1 = reduction (缩小). What does 'enlargement' mean? It means the image is BIGGER than the object (k > 1). For example: k = 2 (double the size), k = 3 (triple the size), k = 4 (four times the size). What does 'reduction' mean? It means the image is SMALLER than the object (0 < k < 1). For example: k = 1/2 (half the size), k = 1/3 (one third the size). Centre of enlargement O: a fixed point. All image points lie on lines from O through the corresponding object points. Image length = k × object length. For example: object side AB = 3 cm, k = 2, then image side A'B' = 2 × 3 = 6 cm. Important: angles do NOT change. If object has ∠X = 40°, then image has ∠X' = 40° (not 40° × k). Scale factor k applies to side LENGTHS only, not to angles. This week: use scale factor k to enlarge or reduce plane figures. Integer lengths only. No calculator.'",
