        teacherNotes: "Ask the child: 'Today we learn determining congruent or similar triangles（判断allwait or similarthreeangle）. What does 'congruent' mean? Congruent means the triangles have the same shape and the same size（allwait: same shape and same size）. If two triangles are congruent, all corresponding sides are equal and all corresponding angles are equal. Example: Triangle ABC has sides 5 cm, 7 cm, 8 cm. Triangle PQR has sides 5 cm, 7 cm, 8 cm. The triangles are congruent by SSS (Side-Side-Side): all three sides are equal, so the triangles are congruent. What does 'similar' mean? Similar means the triangles have the same shape but may have different sizes（similar: same shapenotsamesize）. If two triangles are similar, all corresponding angles are equal and all corresponding sides are proportional (in the same ratio). Example: Triangle DEF has sides 3 cm, 4 cm, 5 cm. Triangle XYZ has sides 6 cm, 8 cm, 10 cm. Check the ratios: 3:6 = 1:2, 4:8 = 1:2, 5:10 = 1:2. All three ratios are the same (1:2), so the sides are proportional. The triangles are similar with scale factor 1:2. Important: Congruent triangles are always similar (special case with scale factor 1:1), but similar triangles are not necessarily congruent (they may have different sizes). Let's review the congruence tests: SSS (three sides equal), SAS (two sides and the included angle equal), ASA (two angles and the included side equal), AAS (two angles and a non-included side equal), RHS (right angle, hypotenuse, one side equal for right-angled triangles). Let's review the similarity tests: AAA (three corresponding angles equal) or three pairs of corresponding sides proportional. Today we practice deciding which test applies and whether the triangles are congruent, similar, or neither.'",
